body: "## Summary\nThe composite claim-and-start path returns two full item snapshots with different statuses, a raw input ID, and no top-level final status or changed-field count. Plain claim follows the compact canonical receipt contract.\n\nReproduced with the official installed `@unbrained/pm-cli` 2026.9.30 on Linux, using disposable synthetic non-Git trackers. No implementation changes are included in this issue.\n\n## Reproduction\n```bash\nLAB=$(mktemp -d)\nmkdir -p \"$LAB/home\" \"$LAB/work\"\nexport HOME=\"$LAB/home\" PM_PATH=\"$LAB/work/.agents/pm\"\nexport PM_GLOBAL_PATH=\"$LAB/global\" PM_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1\ncd \"$LAB/work\"\npm init \"$PM_PATH\" --defaults --author audit --agent-guidance skip --json >/dev/null\npm create task \"Audit sample\" --id t1 --description \"Execution detail\" --json >/dev/null\n```\n```bash\npm claim t1 --start --author audit --json >start.json\npm claim t1 --author audit --json >claim.json\n```\n\n## Actual\nThe start receipt has this shape (abridged):\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"t1\",\n  \"action\": \"start_task\",\n  \"claim\": {\"item\": {\"id\":\"pm-t1\",\"status\":\"open\"}, \"changed_fields\":[\"assignee\",\"claim_principal\"]},\n  \"update\": {\"item\": {\"id\":\"pm-t1\",\"status\":\"in_progress\"}, \"changed_fields\":[\"status\"], \"warnings\":[]}\n}\n```\nBoth nested items contain the full content, not just the excerpt above. In the measured fixture, this was 1,299 bytes. Plain claim returned a 151-byte flat receipt with canonical `id: \"pm-t1\"`, final status and `changed_field_count`. Root help advertises the flat mutation contract.\n\n## Expected and impact\nThe optimized composite command should provide a compact, unambiguous final receipt by default: canonical ID, final status, total changed-field count and concise per-stage outcomes. It should not make consumers guess whether the stale claim snapshot or the update snapshot is authoritative.\n\n## Implementation plan\n1. Route claim/start composite results through the shared mutation receipt normalization.\n2. Resolve the canonical item ID once and propagate it to all top-level and stage references.\n3. Derive final status from the completed operation state; avoid duplicate full item snapshots by default.\n4. Preserve concise claim/update outcomes and explicit partial-failure receipts. Do not hide a successful claim if starting fails, or imply atomicity unless guaranteed.\n5. Keep full stage snapshots and detailed changed fields behind an explicit documented opt-in; account for compatibility consumers and SDK/MCP parity.\n6. Document composite result semantics alongside #1359's execution/checkpoint work.\n\n## Regression acceptance\n- [ ] Short and canonical input IDs produce the same canonical outer ID\n- [ ] Default receipt has final status and changed-field count without duplicate item bodies\n- [ ] Start success, already-owned/no-op, unavailable claim, and update-after-claim failure report truthful stage outcomes\n- [ ] Full-detail opt-in is complete and consistent with the compact receipt\n- [ ] JSON and default rendering share the same semantics\n- [ ] Deterministic payload-size regression catches duplicate snapshot reintroduction; byte counts are not presented as provider tokens\n\n## Ownership and duplicate check\nChild of #1363; related #1359 and #1362 under #1357. Closed #437 established lean mutation defaults; this is the still-uncompacted composite path, including canonical identity and final-state ambiguity. Searched open and closed claim/start/receipt issues; #1352 is a different invalid-flag recovery failure. No implementation is included here."
